#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
#: 4.x/g101/start/index.html
msgid "The Godot Editor: Finding your way around"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "When you&rsquo;re naming your project, try to choose a name that describes the project. &ldquo;New Game Project #23&rdquo; is not going to help you remember what that project was. You should also think about compatibility: some operating systems are case-sensitive, and some are not. This can lead to problems if you move or share your project from one computer to another. For this reason, many programmers develop a standardized naming scheme. For example: &ldquo;No spaces, use &lsquo;_&rsquo; between words.&rdquo;"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Now we&rsquo;ve talked about the main parts of the Godot window and how they work, let&rsquo;s spend a little time talking about our Project settings. Usually one of the first tasks when starting a new project is make sure it&rsquo;s all set up correctly."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Project Manager"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "There are also some tabs across the top. We&rsquo;ve been looking at the General tab. I&rsquo;ll also point out briefly, the Input Map . This is where you can define different input actions for keyboard control, gamepad, mouse, and so on. In your game, you&rsquo;ll just worry about the action, not what individual key or button was pressed. This is a very powerful and flexible way of handling player input."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "The Godot Editor: Finding your way around :: Godot Recipes"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "So let&rsquo;s click on Project in the menu and select Project Settings ."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Playtest Buttons: These buttons let you launch and control your game when testing."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Every Godot project is contained in its own folder. This has many benefits, including making it easy to move, share, and backup projects. It also means that all the project&rsquo;s files (images, sounds, etc.) must be in the project folder."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_01/index.html
#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Getting Started >"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Here you can give the project a name and create a folder to store it in."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "In here, you can set your game&rsquo;s title, choose which scene is the &ldquo;main scene&rdquo; (more about that in a bit), and change the icon."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Workspaces: At the center-top, you can switch between working in the 2D, 3D, or Script workspaces. You start in 3D."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Project Settings"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "You&rsquo;re now looking at the Godot editor window. This is where you&rsquo;ll spend most of your time when working in Godot. The editor is divided into sections."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "In this window you can see a list of your Godot projects. You can choose an existing project and click &ldquo;Run&rdquo; to play the game or click &ldquo;Edit&rdquo; to work on it in the Godot editor. Since you probably don&rsquo;t have any projects yet, let&rsquo;s start by clicking the &ldquo;New Project&rdquo; button."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "We also have localization options, if you plan to support multiple languages. Autoloading, which we&rsquo;ll get to later, and plugins. The Godot community has created a variety of useful plugins that you can download and add to supply more features, different tools, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "The Project Manager is the first thing you&rsquo;ll see when opening Godot."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "This is the Project settings window. On the left is a list of categories. For most projects, the default settings will be fine, and you shouldn&rsquo;t worry about changing them unless you have a very specific need. For now, we&rsquo;re just going to look at two of the sections. First, Application/Config ."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Bottom Panel: Here, you&rsquo;ll see context-specific information for various tools. The most important one to note first is the Output panel, where you&rsquo;ll see any error or informational messages when your game is running."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Second, let&rsquo;s look at the Display section. This is where you set up your game&rsquo;s display. & let you set the size of the game window. If, for example, you were making a mobile game, you&rsquo;d want to set this to the resolution and proportions of your target device. There are also settings for scaling, stretching, fullscreen mode, and more. For now, we&rsquo;ll leave the default size - later on we&rsquo;ll talk about how to adjust these to get our game running on different devices."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "We&rsquo;ll come back to the project settings window later. Let&rsquo;s close it for now and we&rsquo;re ready to move on to the next step: working with nodes."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Viewport: This is where you&rsquo;ll see the parts of your game as you&rsquo;re working on them."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Docks/Tabs: On both sides are a number of docks where you can view game items and set their properties."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_02/index.html
msgid "Let&rsquo;s name this new project &ldquo;getting_started&rdquo;. Type this name, click Create Folder , and then click Create & Edit ."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "In the Inspector, click to expand the &ldquo;Transform&rdquo; section, and type in the Position property."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Nodes are the basic building blocks for creating games in Godot. A node is an object that can represent some kind of specialized game function. A given type of node might display graphics, play an animation, or represent a 3D model of an object. The node also contains a collection of properties, allowing you to customize its behavior. Which nodes you add to your project will depend on what functionality you need. It&rsquo;s a modular system designed to give you flexibility in building your game objects."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Here you&rsquo;ll see the whole hierarchy of node types available in the engine. For example, the nodes with the bluish icons all fall under the &ldquo;Node2D&rdquo; category, meaning they will all have the properties of a Node2D. More about that in a moment."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Scenes"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Over in the viewport, the sprite doesn&rsquo;t look like much. A sprite&rsquo;s purpose is to display an image, or texture. As you can see in the Inspector, the Texture property is currently empty. Fortunately, every new Godot project comes with an image we can use: the Godot icon. Drag the icon from the Filesystem dock and drop it in the texture property."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "One important property of nodes is that they can be arranged in a parent-child hierarchy. Make sure you have the selected and press the add button again. Add another and also drag the icon into its texture."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "You can also click and drag the sprite around in the viewport, and you&rsquo;ll see the Position values changing as you move."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "The list is long, and it would be frustrating to have to drill down every time to find the node you need. Instead, you can use the search function to find it using a small number of characters. We&rsquo;re looking for the node, so I&rsquo;ll just type &ldquo;sp&rdquo; and we&rsquo;ll jump right to it. Click &ldquo;Create&rdquo; to add the node."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Now we have this node in our Scene dock. Make sure it&rsquo;s selected, and then look at the Inspector dock on the right side. Over here, you&rsquo;ll see all the properties of whatever node you have selected. Notice that the properties are organized by where they come from. The node inherits from , which inherits from , which inherits from the plain old ."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Nodes are objects , in the programming sense. They encapsulate data and behavior, and they can inherit properties from other nodes. Rather than use one of the default suggestions, let&rsquo;s click the &ldquo;Add/Create a New Node&rdquo; button in the scene dock."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "A group of nodes arranged in a &ldquo;tree&rdquo; structure like this is called a Scene . In the next part, we&rsquo;ll look at how you can use scenes to organize your game&rsquo;s objects into independent parts that all work together. You&rsquo;ll see this in practice was you work through the examples in later lessons."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Notice that the Position of the parent is changing as you move it around. Now check the child: it&rsquo;s still . That&rsquo;s because its &ldquo;Transform&rdquo; properties are relative to its parent."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Grouping nodes together like this is a powerful tool, enabling you to construct complex objects out of node &ldquo;building blocks&rdquo;. For example, a &ldquo;Player&rdquo; node in your game might have many child nodes attached to it: a for display, an to animate it, a to follow it around, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "Working with Nodes"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/g101/start/101_03/index.html
msgid "This new sprite is a child of the first. This means that it&rsquo;s &ldquo;attached&rdquo; to its parent. If the parent sprite moves, so will the child. Click on the child sprite and set its Position to . Now click and drag the parent sprite to move it around the screen."
msgstr ""

